That does not replace upstream terms. Source records keep the licenses
they were published under:

- MetXBioDB (NORMAN-SLE S73): CC BY 4.0. BioTransformer software is LGPL.
- ChEMBL metabolism records: CC BY-SA 3.0. Share-alike may apply to rows
  derived from ChEMBL.
- SwissPharma24 (S113), HSDBTPS (S68), REFTPS (S74), UACCSCEC (S79) via
  PubChem Transformations / LCSB ECI / NORMAN-SLE: CC BY 4.0.
- LCSB ECI PubChem Transformations merge: CC BY 4.0 (attribution
  University of Luxembourg / LCSB ECI, plus each row's citations).
  XenoBase imports only a mammalian xenobiotic-metabolism subset of that
  merge (not the full environmental / non-mammalian collection); see the
  XenoBase Sources page and How it works.
- PubChem (NCBI/NLM/NIH): US government work; deposited data keeps its
  depositors' terms.
- Hazardous Substances Data Bank (HSDB): US government work.
- EFSA Public MetaPath (Zenodo 10.5281/zenodo.22918585), MamTox maps:
  EFSA confirmed the data are public when properly cited. Cite as:
  European Food Safety Authority (EFSA). (2026). EFSA's Public MetaPath
  database (Version 4) [Dataset]. Zenodo.
  https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22918585
  (Zenodo also lists CC BY 4.0.)

Cite XenoBase and the snapshot you used (git commit or Zenodo version
DOI). Also cite the databases and papers behind each record. See the
XenoBase Sources page.
